'''''SpongeBob SquarePants''''', also known simply as '''''SpongeBob''''', is an American animated comedy television series created by [[marine science]] educator and animator [[Stephen Hillenburg]] for [[Nickelodeon]]. It follows the adventures of [[SpongeBob SquarePants (character)|SpongeBob SquarePants]], an [[Anthropomorphism|anthropomorphic]] yellow [[Sponge|sea sponge]], and his aquatic friends in the underwater city of Bikini Bottom. 

Many of the series' ideas originated in ''The Intertidal Zone'', an educational comic book about undersea life that Hillenburg created in the 1980s. After working on Nickelodeon's ''[[Rocko's Modern Life]]'', he developed ''SpongeBob'' into a television series and pitched it to Nickelodeon in 1997. It first aired as a sneak peek after the [[1999 Kids' Choice Awards|Kids' Choice Awards]] on May&nbsp;1, 1999, and officially premiered on July&nbsp;17, 1999. Hillenburg departed the series after the first three seasons and the production of ''[[The SpongeBob SquarePants Movie]]'' (2004), but returned in 2015 until his death in 2018.

''SpongeBob'' has received widespread critical acclaim since its release, with praise given to its characters, [[Surreal humour|surreal humor]], writing, visuals, animation, and [[Hawaiian music|Hawaiian]]-influenced soundtrack. It quickly became Nickelodeon's highest-rated program and has earned [[List of awards and nominations received by SpongeBob SquarePants|a variety of awards]], including six [[Annie Awards]], eight [[Motion Picture Sound Editors#Golden Reel Awards|Golden Reel Awards]], four [[Emmy Awards]], two [[British Academy Children's Awards|BAFTA Children's Awards]], and a record twenty-two [[Nickelodeon Kids' Choice Awards|Kids' Choice Awards]]. It is often considered to be one of [[List of television shows considered the best|the greatest animated series of all time]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Francisco |first=Eric |date=September 13, 2024 |title=The 32 greatest animated TV shows of all time |url=https://www.gamesradar.com/entertainment/animation-shows/the-32-greatest-animated-tv-shows-of-all-time/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241007182031/https://www.gamesradar.com/entertainment/animation-shows/the-32-greatest-animated-tv-shows-of-all-time/ |archive-date=October 7, 2024 |access-date=June 1, 2025 |website=[[GamesRadar+]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.ign.com/lists/top-100-animated-series |title=Top 100 Animated Series |access-date=June 1, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111223135301/http://tv.ign.com/top-100-animated-tv-series/2.html |archive-date=December 23, 2011 |url-status=dead |website=[[IGN]]}}</ref> The show has been noted as a cultural touchstone for [[Millennials]] and [[Generation Z]],<ref>{{Cite web |title=SpongeBob SquarePants: The Most Important Show to Generation Z and their Popular Culture |url=https://www.bcschronicle.com/spongebob-squarepants-the-most-important-show-to-generation-z-and-their-popular-culture |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230508224949/https://www.bcschronicle.com/spongebob-squarepants-the-most-important-show-to-generation-z-and-their-popular-culture |archive-date=May 8, 2023 |access-date=May 8, 2023 |website=Bryan-College Station Chronicle}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Fuller |first=Benjamin |date=2019 |title=The SpongeBob Franchise: Pop Culture Fixture, Reboot Culture Artifact |journal=Studies in Popular Culture |volume=42 |issue=1 |pages=77–102 |issn=0888-5753 |jstor=26926333}}</ref> and it has spawned numerous [[internet meme]]s.<ref name="Romano-2019">{{Cite web |last=Romano |first=Aja |date=May 1, 2019 |title=20 years into Spongebob Squarepants, Spongebob memes rule internet culture. |url=https://www.vox.com/culture/2019/5/1/18525337/spongebob-memes-mocking-caveman-history |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250528072612/https://www.vox.com/culture/2019/5/1/18525337/spongebob-memes-mocking-caveman-history |archive-date=May 28, 2025 |access-date=June 1, 2025 |website=[[Vox (website)|Vox]]}}</ref>

The series has run for sixteen seasons,<ref>{{cite web |last=Milligan |first=Mercedes |date=June 2, 2025 |title=Nickelodeon Kicks Off "Summer of F.U.N." with New SpongeBob Crossover Special |url=https://animationmagazine.net/2025/06/nickelodeon-kicks-off-summer-of-f-u-n-with-new-spongebob-crossover-special/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250608002138/https://www.animationmagazine.net/2025/06/nickelodeon-kicks-off-summer-of-f-u-n-with-new-spongebob-crossover-special/ |archive-date=June 8, 2025 |access-date=June 7, 2025 |work=[[Animation Magazine]]}}</ref> with its [[SpongeBob SquarePants season 17|seventeenth season]] premiering on June 12, 2026.<ref name="Zap2it Season 17">{{cite web |date=September 2017 |title=''SpongeBob SquarePants'' Season 17 Episode Guide |url=https://tvlistings.gracenote.com/overview-affiliates.html?programSeriesId=SH00307766&tabName=guide&season=17 |publisher=[[Zap2it]]}}</ref> On June 9, 2026, the series was renewed for a sixteenth production cycle, which would consist of the eighteenth and nineteenth seasons.<ref name="SBSPS18S19">{{cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=June 9, 2026 |title=''SpongeBob SquarePants'' & ''The Patrick Star Show'' Renewed For More Seasons By Nickelodeon |url=https://deadline.com/2026/06/spongebob-squarepants-patrick-star-show-renewed-nickelodeon-1236942445/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260609185529/https://deadline.com/2026/06/spongebob-squarepants-patrick-star-show-renewed-nickelodeon-1236942445/ |archive-date=June 9, 2026 |access-date=June 9, 2026 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> ''SpongeBob'' is the [[List of longest-running American television series|fourth longest-running American animated series]] in history, and the longest-running American children's animated series as of 2026.<ref name="Arthur">{{cite web |date=February 22, 2025 |title=SpongeBob Beats Arthur to Become the Longest Running Animated Kids Show |url=https://comicbook.com/anime/news/spongebob-longest-running-animated-shows-kids/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250222224558/https://comicbook.com/anime/news/spongebob-longest-running-animated-shows-kids/ |archive-date=February 22, 2025 |access-date=February 24, 2025 |website=[[ComicBook.com]]}}</ref> Its popularity has made it a [[SpongeBob SquarePants (franchise)|multimedia franchise]] and Nickelodeon's most profitable program, generating over $16{{nbsp}}billion in merchandising revenue by 2024.<ref name=":1" /> The franchise now includes four theatrical feature films, two [[streaming television|streaming]] feature films, a [[SpongeBob SquarePants (musical)|Broadway musical]], a [[SpongeBob Comics|comic book series]], numerous [[SpongeBob SquarePants video games|video games]] and two [[Spinoff (media)|spin-off]] series: ''[[Kamp Koral: SpongeBob's Under Years]]'' (2021–2024) and ''[[The Patrick Star Show]]'' (2021–present).<ref>{{Cite book |title=Children's Software & New Media Revue |publisher=Children's Software & New Media Revue |others=Active Learning Associates |year=2004 |pages=31}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Gold |first=Michael |date=May 2, 2018 |title=Before the Tonys, SpongeBob Seized the Culture With Memes |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/02/arts/spongebob-tony-nominations.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180502234851/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/02/arts/spongebob-tony-nominations.html |archive-date=May 2, 2018 |access-date=May 3, 2018 |newspaper=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref>

==Premise==
===Characters===
{{Main|List of SpongeBob SquarePants characters|l1 = List of ''SpongeBob SquarePants'' characters}}

[[File:SpongeBob SquarePants main characters.png|thumb|300px|alt=Illustration of the series' ten main characters.|The series' main characters. Top row, from left to right: [[Pearl Krabs|Pearl]], [[Sheldon J. Plankton|Plankton]], and [[Karen Plankton|Karen]]. Bottom row: [[Sandy Cheeks|Sandy]], [[Mr. Krabs]], [[SpongeBob SquarePants (character)|SpongeBob]], [[Squidward Tentacles|Squidward]], [[Gary the Snail|Gary]], [[Patrick Star|Patrick]], and [[Mrs. Puff]].]]

The series follows [[SpongeBob SquarePants (character)|SpongeBob SquarePants]]—an energetic and optimistic [[sponge|sea sponge]] who lives in a submerged pineapple—and his aquatic friends. SpongeBob has a childlike enthusiasm for life and works as a [[fry cook]] at the [[Krusty Krab]], a fast food restaurant. He enjoys catching jellyfish and blowing soap bubbles into elaborate shapes, and he hopes to one day obtain a boat-driving license. He has a pet [[sea snail]] named [[List of SpongeBob SquarePants characters#Gary the Snail|Gary]], who meows like a cat.

SpongeBob's best friend is [[Patrick Star]], a dimwitted yet friendly pink [[starfish]] who resides under a rock. Patrick is unaware of his stupidity and considers himself intelligent.<ref>{{cite episode |title=Squidtastic Voyage |series=SpongeBob SquarePants |network=[[Nickelodeon]] |airdate=October 6, 2006 |season=4 |number=75a |credits=Writers: [[Luke Brookshier]], Tom King, Dani Michaeli}}</ref> [[Squidward Tentacles]], SpongeBob's neighbor and co-worker at the Krusty Krab, is a grumpy and cynical octopus who lives in an Easter Island [[moai]]. He despises his job and is constantly annoyed by the antics of SpongeBob and Patrick. [[Mr.&nbsp;Krabs]], a greedy red crab, is the owner of the Krusty Krab and often serves as a father figure to SpongeBob. He has a teenage daughter, a grey [[sperm whale]] named [[Pearl Krabs|Pearl]], who has no interest in taking over the family business.<ref>{{cite web |title=''SpongeBob SquarePants'' at NickSplat |url=http://www.nicksplat.com/Tvshows/Characters/Nicktoons/Spongebob_squarepants/pearl.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040812015604/http://www.nicksplat.com/Tvshows/Characters/Nicktoons/Spongebob_squarepants/pearl.html |archive-date=August 12, 2004 |work=[[Nickelodeon (Asia)|Nickelodeon Asia]] |publisher=[[Viacom International]]}}</ref> Another of SpongeBob's friends is [[Sandy Cheeks]], a thrill-seeking squirrel from Texas, who wears a diving suit to breathe underwater.<ref name="meet-the-gang">{{cite web |title=''SpongeBob SquarePants'': Meet the Gang! |url=http://www.nickelodeon.com.au/fullynick/tvshows/shows/spongebob-squarepants-show |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080310224329/http://www.nickelodeon.com.au/fullynick/tvshows/shows/spongebob-squarepants-show |archive-date=March 10, 2008 |work=[[Nickelodeon (Australia and New Zealand)|Nickelodeon Australia]] |publisher=[[Viacom International]]}}</ref> She is an expert in [[karate]].

Located across the street from the Krusty Krab is an unsuccessful rival restaurant called the Chum Bucket.<ref>{{cite episode |title=Komputer Overload |series=SpongeBob SquarePants |network=[[Nickelodeon]] |airdate=March 19, 2009 |season=6 |number=118b |credits=Writers: [[Aaron Springer]], Richard Pursel}}</ref> It rarely has any customers due to its sale of [[Chumming|chum]]. It is run by a small, green, one-eyed [[copepod]] named [[Plankton (Spongebob)|Plankton]] and his computer wife, [[Karen Plankton|Karen]], who try to steal the Krabby Patty secret recipe.<ref name="Hillenburg created" /><ref>{{cite web |date=July 22, 2016 |title=''SpongeBob SquarePants'' Character Guide Refresh |url=https://issuu.com/dhosford/docs/sb_character_guide |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200807141336/https://issuu.com/dhosford/docs/sb_character_guide |archive-date=August 7, 2020 |access-date=February 19, 2019 |work=Nickelodeon Consumer Products |publisher=[[Viacom International]] |page=3 |quote=Nowhere else could you expect to find a cast that includes a scuba-diving squirrel, Sandy Cheeks, and a waterproof supercomputer, Karen.}}</ref> When SpongeBob is not working at the Krusty Krab, he can often be found taking boating lessons from [[Mrs. Puff]], a paranoid but patient [[pufferfish]]. SpongeBob wants to obtain a boat-driving license, but he often panics and crashes, causing him to fail the course multiple times.<ref>{{cite news |last=Sichtermann |first=Barbara |date=December 4, 2008 |title=SpongeBob: Das Kind im Schwamme |url=http://www.tagesspiegel.de/medien/spongebob-das-kind-im-schwamme/1209130.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190220095452/http://www.tagesspiegel.de/medien/spongebob-das-kind-im-schwamme/1209130.html |archive-date=February 20, 2019 |access-date=November 6, 2016 |work=[[Der Tagesspiegel]] |publisher=[[Dieter von Holtzbrinck]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Mrs. Puff at Nickelodeon Universe |url=http://nickelodeonuniverse.com/character/mrs-puff/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170606214926/http://nickelodeonuniverse.com/character/mrs-puff/ |archive-date=June 6, 2017 |work=[[Mall of America]] |publisher=[[Triple Five Group]]}}</ref>

The French Narrator, or "Frenchy", is an unseen narrator who often introduces episodes and narrates [[intertitle]]s as if the series were a documentary about the ocean. His role and manner of speaking are references to the oceanographer [[Jacques Cousteau]].<ref name="Square Roots">{{cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lT7VzFW0acE |title=Square Roots: The Story of ''SpongeBob SquarePants'' |date=July 14, 2009 |time=9:29 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211221/lT7VzFW0acE |archive-date=December 21, 2021 |url-status=live}}{{cbignore}}</ref> Beginning in season{{nbsp}}10, the narrator has appeared infrequently in [[Live action|live-action]] as a diver in a [[Deep sea diving suit|heavy deep sea diving suit]].

Recurring guest characters include the retired superheroes [[Mermaid Man and Barnacle Boy]], who are idolized by SpongeBob and Patrick; a pirate specter known as the [[List of SpongeBob SquarePants characters#The Flying Dutchman|Flying Dutchman]]; the muscular lifeguard [[List of SpongeBob SquarePants characters#Larry the Lobster|Larry the Lobster]]; and the [[merman]] god of the sea, [[List of SpongeBob SquarePants characters#King Neptune|King Neptune]].

Special episodes of the show are hosted by a live-action pirate named [[Patchy the Pirate|Patchy]] and his pet parrot [[Potty the Parrot|Potty]], whose segments are presented in a [[dual narrative]] with the animated stories.<ref>{{cite news |author=Bianco, Robert |date=March 21, 2003 |title=Critic's corner |newspaper=[[USA Today]] |page=12E}}</ref> Patchy is depicted as the president of a fictional SpongeBob fan club, and his greatest aspiration is to meet SpongeBob himself.

===Setting===
{{Redirect|Bikini Bottom}}
[[File:Bikini Bottom.png|thumb|right|A still image of Bikini Bottom from the series]]

The series takes place primarily in the fictional underwater city of '''Bikini Bottom''' located in the [[Pacific Ocean]] beneath the real-life [[coral reef]] known as [[Bikini Atoll]].{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{cite web |author=Viacom International |title=Nickelodeon UK & Ireland Press Centre: ''SpongeBob SquarePants'' Programme Information |url=http://www.vimn.com/press/nicktoons/series/spongebob-squarepants-0 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170315035655/http://www.vimn.com/press/nicktoons/series/spongebob-squarepants-0 |archive-date=March 15, 2017 |access-date=May 4, 2018 |work=[[Nickelodeon]]}}</ref><ref name="LADN">{{cite web |date=March 8, 2001 |title=The Hype Soaking it Up' SpongeBob Actor Loves the Attention |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-83498648.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131106075350/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-83498648.html |archive-date=November 6, 2013 |access-date=October 30, 2013 |work=Daily News |via=[[HighBeam Research]] |url-access=subscription |url-status=usurped |location=Los Angeles, CA}}</ref><ref name="QSR">{{Cite news |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=June 7, 2001 |title=Burger King ''SpongeBob SquarePants'' |url=http://www.qsrmagazine.com/articles/news/story.phtml?id=3354 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071021075539/http://www.qsrmagazine.com/articles/news/story.phtml?id=3354 |archive-date=October 21, 2007 |work=QSR Magazine}}</ref>}} Its citizens are mostly multicolored fish who live in buildings made from ship funnels, and use "boatmobiles" for transportation. Recurring locations within Bikini Bottom include the neighboring houses of SpongeBob, Patrick, and Squidward; two competing restaurants, the Krusty Krab and the [[Plankton and Karen|Chum Bucket]]; Mrs. Puff's Boating School, which includes a driving course and a sunken [[lighthouse]]; the Treedome, an oxygenated glass enclosure where Sandy lives; Shady Shoals Rest Home; a [[seagrass meadow]] called Jellyfish Fields; and Goo Lagoon, a subaqueous [[brine pool]] that is a popular beach hangout.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Grandy |first1=Trevor |title=The Geology of ''SpongeBob SquarePants'': Potential of a Cartoon to Enhance Student Learning in the Geosciences |url=https://gsa.confex.com/gsa/2013AM/webprogram/Paper223766.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170606214926/http://nickelodeonuniverse.com/character/mrs-puff/ |archive-date=June 6, 2017 |access-date=September 5, 2019 |publisher=[[Geological Society of America]]}}</ref>

When the ''SpongeBob'' crew began production of the series' [[pilot episode]], they were tasked with designing stock locations where many scenes would take place. The idea was "to keep everything nautical", so the crew included objects like ropes, wooden planks, ships' wheels, netting, anchors, boilerplates, and rivets. Transitions between scenes are marked by bubbles filling the screen, accompanied by the sound of rushing water. The settings also feature "sky flowers", which have a similar function to clouds, according to background designer Kenny Pittenger. The sky flowers are also meant to "evoke the look of a flower-print [[Aloha shirt|Hawaiian shirt]]".<ref name="OralHistory">{{cite web |author=Heintjes, Tom |date=September 21, 2012 |title=The Oral History of ''SpongeBob SquarePants'' |url=http://cartoonician.com/the-oral-history-of-spongebob-squarepants/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150831044034/http://cartoonician.com/the-oral-history-of-spongebob-squarepants/ |archive-date=August 31, 2015 |work=[[Hogan's Alley (magazine)|Hogan's Alley]]}}</ref>

In 2015, [[Tom Kenny]], who voices SpongeBob, denied a fan theory claiming that the series' characters are mutants who were exposed to [[nuclear testing at Bikini Atoll]].<ref name=":0">{{cite news |last=Bradley |first=Bill |date=February 7, 2015 |title=SpongeBob SquarePants Answers 7 Big Questions And Debunks 1 Popular Theory |url=https://www.huffingtonpost.com/2015/02/07/spongebob-squarepants-theory_n_6627556.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170907053320/http://www.huffingtonpost.com/2015/02/07/spongebob-squarepants-theory_n_6627556.html |archive-date=September 7, 2017 |access-date=July 10, 2016 |work=[[The Huffington Post]]}}</ref> In 2024, Plankton voice actor [[Mr. Lawrence]] said the theory is true, and that the real-life nuclear testing influenced other aspects of the show as well.<ref>{{cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5hlE75E4Yz8 |title=SpongeBob Cast Reunites To Talk 25 Years of Krabby Patties and F-U-N |date=November 27, 2024 |publisher=[[YouTube]] |access-date=January 14, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241219164049/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5hlE75E4Yz8 |archive-date=December 19, 2024 |url-status=live |people=[[Esquire (magazine)|Esquire]]}}</ref>

==Production==
===Development===
====Early inspirations====
[[File:Stephen Hillenburg by Carlos Cazurro.jpg|thumb|upright|right| alt=Photograph of Stephen Hillenburg standing holding a book with the title SpongeBob SquarePants looking to his right | Before creating ''SpongeBob'', Stephen Hillenburg (pictured in 2011) taught marine biology to visitors of the [[Ocean Institute]] at [[Dana Point, California]].<ref name="HillenburgWaPo" />]]
Series creator [[Stephen Hillenburg]] became fascinated with the ocean as a child and began developing his artistic abilities at a young age.<ref name="HillenburgWaPo">{{cite news |last=Schudel |first=Matt |date=November 27, 2018 |title='SpongeBob SquarePants' creator Stephen Hillenburg dies at 57 |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/obituaries/spongebob-squarepants-creator-stephen-hillenburg-dies-at-57/2018/11/27/9b3d4a80-f270-11e8-bc79-68604ed88993_story.html |access-date=August 24, 2026 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref> Although these interests would not overlap for some time—the idea of drawing fish seemed boring to him—Hillenburg pursued both during college, majoring in [[marine biology]] and minoring in art at [[Humboldt State University]].<ref name="Big Pop Fun">{{cite interview |last=Hillenburg |first=Stephen |subject-link=Stephen Hillenburg |interviewer=[[Thomas F. Wilson]] |title=Big Pop Fun #28: Stephen Hillenburg, Artist and Animator |medium=Podcast |url=https://archive.nerdist.com/big-pop-fun-28-stephen-hillenburg-artist-and-animator/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260227040505/https://archive.nerdist.com/big-pop-fun-28-stephen-hillenburg-artist-and-animator/ |archive-date=February 27, 2026 |format=mp3 |work=[[Nerdist Industries]] |date=May 25, 2012 |access-date=April 10, 2026}}</ref> After graduating in 1984, he joined the [[Ocean Institute]], an organization in [[Dana Point, California]], dedicated to educating the public about [[marine science]] and [[maritime history]].<ref name="HillenburgWaPo" /><ref name="Ocean Institute">{{cite web |title=Our Story |url=https://oceaninstitute.org/oitoday/ |access-date=August 24, 2026 |website=Ocean Institute}}</ref>

While Hillenburg was working at the Ocean Institute, his love of the ocean began to influence his art. He created a comic book precursor to ''SpongeBob'' titled ''The Intertidal Zone'', which was used by the institute to teach visitors about the [[Tide pool#Marine life|animal life of tide pools]].<ref name="Big Pop Fun"/> The comic featured various [[Anthropomorphism|anthropomorphic]] lifeforms, many of which would evolve into ''SpongeBob'' characters.{{sfn |Banks |2004 |page=9}} Hillenburg tried to get the comic published, but none of the companies he sent it to were interested.<ref name="Big Pop Fun"/>

One of Hillenburg's major inspirations was [[Ween]]'s 1997 album ''[[The Mollusk]]'', which had a nautical and underwater theme. Hillenburg contacted the band shortly after the album's release, explaining the baseline ideas for ''SpongeBob''. He also commissioned a song, "Loop de Loop", which was used in the ''SpongeBob'' episode "Your Shoe's Untied".{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref>{{cite web |last=O'Brien |first=Andrew |date=March 7, 2019 |title=Did You Know: Ween's 'The Mollusk' Helped Inspire The Creation Of SpongeBob Squarepants |url=https://liveforlivemusic.com/news/ween-spongebob-squarepants-mollusk/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191204024238/https://liveforlivemusic.com/news/ween-spongebob-squarepants-mollusk/ |archive-date=December 4, 2019 |access-date=December 4, 2019 |website=Live for Live Music}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Ween – Tribute to Stephen Hillenburg |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bj2d3ZpumBM |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190826133635/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bj2d3ZpumBM&gl=US&hl=en |archive-date=August 26, 2019 |access-date=December 4, 2019 |website=[[YouTube]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=McQuade |first1=Jakob |date=March 7, 2019 |title=A Deep Dive Into Ween, the Band that Birthed Bikini Bottom |url=http://kcpr.org/2019/03/07/a-deep-dive-into-ween-the-band-that-birthed-bikini-bottom/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190307144216/http://kcpr.org/2019/03/07/a-deep-dive-into-ween-the-band-that-birthed-bikini-bottom/ |archive-date=March 7, 2019 |access-date=December 4, 2019 |newspaper=KCPR}}</ref>}}

====Concept====
While working as a staff artist at the Ocean Institute, Hillenburg intended to return to college for a master's degree in art. However, after attending an animation festival, he decided to instead study experimental animation at [[California Institute of the Arts]].<ref name="Big Pop Fun" /> His thesis film, ''Wormholes'', is about the [[theory of relativity]].<ref name="Origin">{{cite AV media |title=The Origin of ''SpongeBob SquarePants''. ''SpongeBob SquarePants'': The Complete First Season |date=2003 |last1=Hillenburg |first1=Stephen |last2=Murray |first2=Joe |last3=Drymon |first3=Derek |last4=Coleman |first4=Eric |last5=Hecht |first5=Albie |medium=DVD |publisher=Paramount Home Entertainment}}</ref> It was screened at festivals, and at one of these, Hillenburg met [[Joe Murray (animator)|Joe Murray]], who was in the process of developing the popular [[Nickelodeon]] animated series ''[[Rocko's Modern Life]]''. Murray was impressed by the style of Hillenburg's film and offered him a job as a director for the first season, which began airing in 1993.<ref name="Origin" />{{Sfn |Neuwirth |2003 |pp=50–51}} Hillenburg worked on the series throughout its entire four-season run, and during the [[Put to Pasture/Future Schlock|fourth season]] he took on the roles of producer and [[creative director]].{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:{{sfn |Banks |2004 |page=9}}<ref name="Origin"/>{{Sfn |Neuwirth |2003 |pp=50–51}}<ref name="TrainorMurray">{{cite interview |interviewer=Lisa Kiczuk Trainor |title=Lisa (Kiczuk) Trainor interviews Joe Murray, creator of Rocko's Modern Life |url=http://www.title14.com/rocko/contributors/murray.html |access-date=November 5, 2009 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20150420193919/http://www.title14.com/rocko/contributors/murray.html |archive-date=April 20, 2015 |url-status=live |work=The Rocko's Modern Life FAQ |subject=Joe Murray}}</ref>}}

[[Martin Olson]], one of the writers for ''Rocko's Modern Life'', read ''The Intertidal Zone'' and encouraged Hillenburg to create a television series with a similar concept. At that point, Hillenburg had not considered creating his own series.{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref name="Origin" /><ref name="Big Pop Fun" />{{sfn |Banks |2004 |page=10}}}} He began to develop some of the characters from ''The Intertidal Zone'', including Bob the Sponge. He wanted his series to stand out from other popular cartoons of the time, many of which were [[Buddy comedy|buddy comedies]] like ''[[The Ren & Stimpy Show]]''. Hillenburg decided to focus on a single main character: a sea sponge, the "weirdest" sea creature he could think of.<ref name="Big Pop Fun" /> Bob the Sponge resembled a real-life sea sponge, and Hillenburg used this design in the early stages.{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref name="Big Pop Fun" /><ref name="Origin"/>{{Sfn |Neuwirth |2003 |pp=50–51}}{{sfn |Banks |2004 |page=31}}}} He decided to model the character after a kitchen sponge after realizing it would perfectly match the character's "square" personality.{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref name="Origin" /><ref name="Big Pop Fun" />{{Sfn |Neuwirth |2003 |pp=50–51}}<ref name="BoytoBob">{{cite magazine |date=June 2003 |title=From Boy to Bob |magazine=[[Nickelodeon Magazine|Nick Mag Presents: SpongeBob SquarePants]] |publisher=[[Viacom International]]}}</ref>}} In determining the character's behavior, Hillenburg was inspired by innocent, childlike figures that he enjoyed, such as [[Charlie Chaplin]], [[Laurel and Hardy]], [[Jerry Lewis]], and [[Pee-wee Herman]].{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref name="Big Pop Fun" />{{Sfn |Neuwirth |2003 |pp=50–51}}{{sfn |Banks |2004 |page=30}}<ref name="OralHistory"/>}}

Seeking a voice actor for the series' main character, Hillenburg approached Tom Kenny, whose career in animation had started alongside Hillenburg's on ''Rocko's Modern Life''. Elements of Kenny's own personality were employed to develop the character further.<ref name="Radio">{{cite video |url=http://www.thepeoplespeakradio.net/archives/mp3/tps-2006-12-05-kenny.mp3 |title=Tom Kenny: Voice of ''SpongeBob SquarePants'' – Interview |date=December 5, 2006 |medium=Radio production |publisher=The People Speak Radio |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110724101824/http://www.thepeoplespeakradio.net/archives/mp3/tps-2006-12-05-kenny.mp3 |archive-date=July 24, 2011 |format=mp3 |people=Farhat, Basima (Interviewer)}}</ref> Initially, Hillenburg wanted to use the name "SpongeBoy"—the character had no last name—and the series would have been named ''SpongeBoy Ahoy!''&thinsp;{{sfn |Banks |2004 |page=31}}<ref name="Radio" /> However, the Nickelodeon legal department discovered that the name "SpongeBoy" was already trademarked and in use by a commercial mop product.{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref name="OralHistory" /><ref name="Radio" /><ref name="NPRHillenburgObit">{{cite news |last=Kennedy |first=Merrit |date=November 27, 2018 |title=Stephen Hillenburg, Creator Of 'SpongeBob SquarePants,' Has Died At 57 |url=https://www.npr.org/2018/11/27/671247547/stephen-hillenburg-creator-of-spongebob-squarepants-has-died-at-57 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181127181042/https://www.npr.org/2018/11/27/671247547/stephen-hillenburg-creator-of-spongebob-squarepants-has-died-at-57 |archive-date=November 27, 2018 |access-date=July 5, 2026 |publisher=[[NPR]]}}</ref>}} In choosing a replacement name, Hillenburg felt he still had to use the word "Sponge", so that viewers would not mistake the character for a "cheese man". He settled on the name "SpongeBob". The surname "SquarePants" was chosen after Kenny saw a picture of the character and remarked, "Boy, look at this sponge in square pants, thinking he can get a job in a fast food place." When he heard Kenny say it, Hillenburg loved the phrase and felt it would reinforce the character's nerdiness.{{sfn |Banks |2004 |page=30}}{{sfn |Neuwirth |2003 |page=51}}

====Assembling the crew====
Many of the major contributors to ''SpongeBob'' had previously worked with Hillenburg on ''Rocko's Modern Life'', including creative director [[Derek Drymon]], art director [[Nick Jennings (artist)|Nick Jennings]], supervising director [[Alan Smart]], writer and voice actor [[Mr. Lawrence]], and [[Tim Hill (director)|Tim Hill]], who helped develop the [[series bible]]. Although Drymon would go on to have a significant influence on ''SpongeBob'', he was not offered a role on the series initially. As a late recruit to ''Rocko's'' ''Modern Life'', he had not established a strong relationship with Hillenburg prior to ''SpongeBob''{{'}}s development. Hillenburg first sought to recruit Drymon's storyboard partner, [[Mark O'Hare]], but he had recently created the soon-to-be syndicated comic strip, ''[[Citizen Dog (comic)|Citizen Dog]]'', and lacked the time to get involved with ''SpongeBob'', although he would later join the series as a writer. Drymon begged Hillenburg for a job, and after Hillenburg gave it some thought, he brought Drymon on as creative director. The two began meeting at Hillenburg's house several times a week to develop the series. According to Drymon, this occurred in 1996, shortly after the end of ''Rocko's Modern Life''.<ref name="OralHistory" />

Nick Jennings was also instrumental in ''SpongeBob''{{'}}s genesis, and was described by Kenny as an "early graphics mentor".<ref name="OralHistory"/> On weekends, Kenny joined Hillenburg, Jennings, and Drymon for creative sessions where they recorded ideas on a tape recorder. Kenny performed audio tests as SpongeBob during these sessions, while Hillenburg voiced the other characters.{{sfn |Banks |2004 |page=31}}<ref name="OralHistory"/> Tim Hill contributed scripts for the pilot and several first-season episodes, and was offered the role of [[story editor]]. He turned down the offer, and Pete Burns was hired instead.{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref>{{cite episode |title=[[Help Wanted (SpongeBob SquarePants)|Help Wanted]] |series=SpongeBob SquarePants |network=Nickelodeon |airdate=May 1, 1999 |season=1 |number=1a |credits=Writers: Stephen Hillenburg, Derek Drymon, Tim Hill}}</ref><ref>{{cite episode |title=Reef Blower |series=SpongeBob SquarePants |network=Nickelodeon |airdate=May 1, 1999 |season=1 |number=1b |credits=Writers: Stephen Hillenburg, Derek Drymon, Tim Hill}}</ref><ref>{{cite episode |title=Bubble Stand |series=SpongeBob SquarePants |network=Nickelodeon |airdate=July 17, 1999 |season=1 |number=2a |credits=Writers: Ennio Torresan, Erik Wiese, Stephen Hillenburg, Derek Drymon, and Tim Hill}}</ref><ref>{{cite episode |title=Jellyfishing |series=SpongeBob SquarePants |network=Nickelodeon |airdate=July 31, 1999 |season=1 |number=3a |credits=Writers: Steve Fonti, Chris Mitchell, Peter Burns, and Tim Hill}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Tim Hill biography |url=https://www.tribute.ca/people/tim-hill/3103/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110412101523/https://www.tribute.ca/people/tim-hill/3103/ |archive-date=April 12, 2011 |access-date=July 2, 2017 |work=tribute.ca |publisher=[[Tribute (magazine)|Tribute]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=About – Our Artists – Tim Hill |url=http://skylighttheatrecompany.com/about-us/our-artists/536-tim-hill-the-owners-closet.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170731015838/http://skylighttheatrecompany.com/about-us/our-artists/536-tim-hill-the-owners-closet.html |archive-date=July 31, 2017 |access-date=July 2, 2017 |work=Skylight Theatre Company}}</ref><ref name="OralHistory" />}}

====Pitching====
When [[Pitch (filmmaking)|pitching]] the idea for the series to Nickelodeon executives, Drymon said the ''SpongeBob'' crew "went all out" because they thought the pilot would only be accepted if the executives laughed. Hillenburg wore a Hawaiian shirt, brought along a [[Vivarium|terrarium]] containing models of the characters, and played Hawaiian music to set the tone. Executive [[Eric Coleman (producer)|Eric Coleman]] described the pitch as "pretty amazing"; his colleagues [[Kevin Kay]] and [[Albie Hecht]] had to step outside because they were exhausted from laughing. Drymon expected the executives to take weeks to make a decision, but they offered to fund the pilot episode immediately. The crew was given two weeks to write the pilot, titled "[[Help Wanted (SpongeBob SquarePants)|Help Wanted]]".<ref name="Origin" />

Before commissioning the full series, Nickelodeon insisted that it would only be popular if SpongeBob was a child who went to school. The studio felt their popular shows, such as ''[[Hey Arnold!]]'', demonstrated that this approach was a formula for success.<ref name="tbivision">{{cite web |last=White |first=Peter |date=October 27, 2009 |title=SpongeBob SquarePants' creator Steve Hillenburg |url=https://tbivision.com/2009/10/27/spongebob-squarepants-creator-steve-hillenburg/7414/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210511213743/https://tbivision.com/2009/10/27/spongebob-squarepants-creator-steve-hillenburg/7414/ |archive-date=May 11, 2021 |access-date=October 7, 2023 |website=Television Business International |publisher=[[Informa|Informa Telecoms & Media]]}}</ref> Hillenburg was ready to abandon the series, since he wanted SpongeBob to be an adult, but he compromised by adding a new character, Mrs. Puff, a boat-driving teacher whose school SpongeBob would attend. Hillenburg was satisfied with the compromise and happy that it brought in a new character.{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref name="Big Pop Fun" /><ref name="Time Special">{{Cite magazine |last1=Sands |first1 =Rich |last2=Murphy |first2=Emma |last3=Nashawaty |first3=Chris |last4=Canfield |first4=David |last5=Rigaud |first5=Debbie |last6=Intreglia |first6=Courtney Mifsud |last7=Daspin |first7=Eileen |last8=Hogan |first8=Kate |last9=C |first9=Paris |last10=Greenspan |first10=Rachel E. |last11=Colman |first11=David |url=http://archive.org/details/time-sponge-bob-square-pants-the-global-icon-2025 |title=TIME Special Edition - SpongeBob SquarePants: The Global Icon |date=2025 |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]|isbn=978-1-5478-6937-4|quote=Mrs. Puff was added to the cast because Nickelodeon wanted SpongeBob to have a teacher of some sort... the network was hesitant to green-light a series in which the main character was not a student.}}</ref><ref name="Art of an Undersea World">{{Cite book |last=Miller-Zarneke |first=Tracey |url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/SpongeBob_SquarePants_The_Art_of_an_Unde/-au7EQAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22having+SpongeBob+be+a+student%22&pg=PA46&printsec=frontcover |title=SpongeBob SquarePants: The Art of an Undersea World |date=2026-03-03 |publisher=IDW Publishing |isbn=979-8-88724-441-9 |language=en |quote=Mrs. Puff...exists out of creative compromise: She was added to the Bikini Bottom cast of characters when having SpongeBob be a student became a studio mandate for series approval.}}</ref>}}

===Executive producers and showrunners===
Hillenburg served as the executive producer of the series from its debut in 1999 until his death in 2018. He was its [[showrunner]] from 1999 until 2004. Hillenburg halted production of the show in 2002 to work on the feature film ''[[The SpongeBob SquarePants Movie]]'', which he intended to be the [[series finale]]. However, the show's massive financial success led to its continuation.<ref name="OralHistory" /> Hillenburg appointed [[Paul Tibbitt]], who had previously served as a writer, director, and storyboard artist, to take over as showrunner.<ref name="Tibbitt">{{cite web |last=Fletcher |first=Alex |date=April 3, 2011 |title=Paul Tibbitt ('SpongeBob SquarePants') |url=http://www.digitalspy.co.uk/tv/interviews/a312387/paul-tibbitt-spongebob-squarepants.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131031235346/http://www.digitalspy.co.uk/tv/interviews/a312387/paul-tibbitt-spongebob-squarepants.html |archive-date=October 31, 2013 |access-date=May 25, 2013 |work=[[Digital Spy]]}}</ref> He considered Tibbitt one of his favorite crew members, and deeply trusted him.<ref name="Hilleburg1">{{cite video |title=The First 100 Episodes — Square Roots: The Story of ''SpongeBob SquarePants'' |date=2009 |medium=DVD |publisher=[[Paramount Home Entertainment]] |people=Hillenburg, Stephen}}</ref><ref name="Childhood" /> Although Hillenburg no longer had a direct role in the series' production, he maintained an advisory role and reviewed each episode.<ref name="Huffington">{{cite news |last=Bauder |first=David |date=July 13, 2009 |title=SpongeBob Turns 10 Valued At $8 Billion |url=https://www.huffingtonpost.com/2009/07/14/spongebob-turns-10-valued_n_231304.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160208163834/http://www.huffingtonpost.com/2009/07/14/spongebob-turns-10-valued_n_231304.html |archive-date=February 8, 2016 |work=[[Huffington Post]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=July 13, 2009 |title=Nickelodeon's 'SpongeBob SquarePants' Reaches A Milestone: 10 Years |url=http://www.accesshollywood.com/nickelodeons-spongebob-squarepants-reaches-a-milestone-10-years_article_20514 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141030154235/http://www.accesshollywood.com/nickelodeons-spongebob-squarepants-reaches-a-milestone-10-years_article_20514 |archive-date=October 30, 2014 |access-date=May 25, 2013 |work=[[Access Hollywood]]}}</ref>

In December 2014, it was announced that Hillenburg would return to the series in an unspecified position.<ref>{{cite web |last=Amidi |first=Amid |author-link=Amid Amidi |date=December 13, 2014 |title='SpongeBob' Creator Stephen Hillenburg Returning to His Show |url=http://www.cartoonbrew.com/tv/spongebob-creator-stephen-hillenburg-returning-to-his-show-106935.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141214083136/http://www.cartoonbrew.com/tv/spongebob-creator-stephen-hillenburg-returning-to-his-show-106935.html |archive-date=December 14, 2014 |access-date=December 17, 2014 |work=[[Cartoon Brew]]}}</ref> In March 2017, he was diagnosed with [[amyotrophic lateral sclerosis]] (ALS), and on November&nbsp;26, 2018, at the age of 57, he died of complications from the disease.{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref name="Variety">{{cite web |last=Otterson |first=Joe |date=November 27, 2018 |title='SpongeBob Squarepants' Creator Stephen Hillenburg Dies at 57 |url=https://variety.com/2018/tv/news/spongebob-squarepants-creator-dead-dies-stephen-hillenburg-1203037362/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181201084613/https://variety.com/2018/tv/news/spongebob-squarepants-creator-dead-dies-stephen-hillenburg-1203037362/ |archive-date=December 1, 2018 |access-date=November 26, 2018 |work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Wallenstein |first=Andrew |date=March 13, 2017 |title='SpongeBob SquarePants' Creator Stephen Hillenburg Reveals ALS Diagnosis |url=https://variety.com/2017/tv/news/spongebob-squarepants-creator-stephen-hillenburg-reveals-als-diagnosis-1202007865/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170314030811/http://variety.com/2017/tv/news/spongebob-squarepants-creator-stephen-hillenburg-reveals-als-diagnosis-1202007865/ |archive-date=March 14, 2017 |access-date=November 27, 2018 |magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite tweet |number=1067913022822334464 |user=Nickelodeon |title=The show isn't cancelled. Steve Hillenburg's creations will continue to bring joy to kids and families everywhere 💛 |author=Nickelodeon |author-link=Nickelodeon |date=November 28, 2018 |access-date=July 21, 2019 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/X8mWt |archive-date=October 31, 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref>}} In February 2019, incoming Nickelodeon president [[Brian Robbins]] vowed to keep ''SpongeBob'' in production for as long as the network exists.<ref name="brianrobbins">{{cite web |last=Steinberg |first=Brian |date=February 14, 2019 |title='SpongeBob' Spinoffs Planned as Nickelodeon Chief Brian Robbins Tries to Win Back Young Viewers (EXCLUSIVE) |url=https://variety.com/2019/tv/news/nickelodeon-brian-robbins-spongebob-lego-paddington-john-cena-1203139534/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190214214303/https://variety.com/2019/tv/news/nickelodeon-brian-robbins-spongebob-lego-paddington-john-cena-1203139534/ |archive-date=February 14, 2019 |access-date=February 20, 2019 |work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]}}</ref> As of 2024, former writers and storyboard directors [[Vincent Waller]] and [[Marc Ceccarelli (animator)|Marc Ceccarelli]] are serving as the series' showrunners.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Davis |first=Victoria |date=July 17, 2024 |title=Vincent Waller and Marc Ceccarelli Talk 25 Years of ''SpongeBob SquarePants'' |url=https://www.awn.com/animationworld/vincent-waller-and-marc-ceccarelli-talk-25-years-spongebob-squarepants |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251227051210/https://www.awn.com/animationworld/vincent-waller-and-marc-ceccarelli-talk-25-years-spongebob-squarepants |archive-date=December 27, 2025 |access-date=2026-02-20 |website=Animation World Network |language=en}}</ref>

===Writing===
According to writer and storyboard artist [[Luke Brookshier]], ''SpongeBob'' is written differently from many other television shows—it does not use written scripts.<ref name="Producing">{{cite web |date=September 23, 2009 |title=Interview with Luke Brookshier, ''SpongeBob SquarePants'' Storyboard Director |url=http://www.4mations.tv/2009/09/interview-with-luke-brookshier-spongebob-squarepants-storyboard-director/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120323112314/http://www.4mations.tv/2009/09/interview-with-luke-brookshier-spongebob-squarepants-storyboard-director/ |archive-date=March 23, 2012 |access-date=May 23, 2013 |publisher=[[4mations|4Mations]]}}</ref><ref name="StevenB">{{cite web |last=Zahed |first=Ramin |date=July 24, 2009 |title=Bikini Bottom Confessions |url=http://www.animationmagazine.net/tv/bikini-bottom-confessions/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110315064118/http://www.animationmagazine.net/tv/bikini-bottom-confessions/ |archive-date=March 15, 2011 |access-date=May 28, 2013 |work=[[Animation Magazine]]}}</ref> Instead, storylines are developed by a team of five "outline and premise" writers. A two-page outline is then assigned to a team of storyboard directors, who produce a complete rough draft of the storyboard. Most of the dialogue and jokes are added during this stage.<ref name="OralHistory"/><ref name=Producing/> Brookshier has compared this process to how cartoons were made in the early days of animation.<ref name="Producing"/> Almost every episode is divided into two 11-minute segments, due to Hillenburg's belief that ''SpongeBob'' works better in short-form than in long-form.<ref name="Childhood" /> The writing staff often used their personal experiences as inspiration for storylines.<ref name="OralHistory" /><ref name="Childhood">{{cite news |last=Cavna |first=Michael |date=July 14, 2009 |title=The Interview: 'SpongeBob' Creator Stephen Hillenburg |url=http://voices.washingtonpost.com/comic-riffs/2009/07/_tom_kenny_who_voices.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121006111645/http://voices.washingtonpost.com/comic-riffs/2009/07/_tom_kenny_who_voices.html |archive-date=October 6, 2012 |access-date=May 25, 2013 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref> For example, the episode "[[Sailor Mouth]]", in which SpongeBob and Patrick learn [[profanity]] and begin to swear profusely, was inspired by Drymon's childhood experience of getting into trouble for using the word "[[fuck]]" in front of his mother.<ref name="OralHistory" /><ref name="Childhood" />

