During her audition for Pearl, Lori Alan was shown an early drawing of the characters and noted that Pearl was much larger than the others. She decided to express the character's size by making her voice deep and full in tone, while also incorporating the low sound of whale vocalizations. At the same time, Alan sought to make the teenage Pearl sound childlike, spoiled, and lovable.<ref name="AfterBuzzInterview">{{cite interview |last=Alan |first=Lori |subject-link=Lori Alan |interviewer=Kaori Takee |title=Lori Alan Interview |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v1xqehTUUZw |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211221/v1xqehTUUZw |archive-date=December 21, 2021 |url-status=live |work=[[AfterBuzz TV]] |date=November 2015}}</ref>

In addition to the main cast, some episodes feature guest voice performers. Recurring guest performers include [[Ernest Borgnine]] as Mermaid Man (1999–2012), [[Tim Conway]] as Barnacle Boy (1999–2019), [[Brian Doyle-Murray]] as the Flying Dutchman and [[Marion Ross]] as Grandma SquarePants.{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref>{{cite news |last=Lloyd |first=Robert |date=July 9, 2012 |title=Ernest Borgnine: From Marty to McHale to Mermaid Man |url=https://www.latimes.com/entertainment/tv/la-xpm-2012-jul-09-la-et-st-ernest-borgnine-from-marty-to-mchale-to-mermaid-man-20120709-story.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181024025247/http://articles.latimes.com/2012/jul/09/entertainment/la-et-st-ernest-borgnine-from-marty-to-mchale-to-mermaid-man-20120709 |archive-date=October 24, 2018 |access-date=May 2, 2013 |work=[[Los Angeles Times]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Tim Conway: Credits |url=https://www.tvguide.com/celebrities/tim-conway/credits/176843 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080726180604/https://www.tvguide.com/celebrities/tim-conway/credits/176843 |archive-date=July 26, 2008 |access-date=May 22, 2013 |work=[[TV Guide]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Brian Doyle-Murray: Credits |url=https://www.tvguide.com/celebrities/brian-doyle-murray/credits/142746 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090327064020/https://www.tvguide.com/celebrities/brian-doyle-murray/credits/142746 |archive-date=March 27, 2009 |access-date=May 22, 2013 |work=[[TV Guide]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Marion Ross: Credits |url=https://www.tvguide.com/celebrities/marion-ross/credits/164469 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100304103316/https://www.tvguide.com/celebrities/marion-ross/credits/164469 |archive-date=March 4, 2010 |access-date=May 22, 2013 |work=[[TV Guide]]}}</ref>}} Notable guests with cameo appearances include [[David Bowie]] as Lord Royal Highness,{{efn|In the television film ''[[Atlantis SquarePantis]]''}} [[John Goodman]] as Santa,{{efn|In the episode "[[It's a SpongeBob Christmas!]]"}} [[Johnny Depp]] as the surf guru Jack Kahuna Laguna,{{efn|In the episode "[[SpongeBob SquarePants vs. The Big One]]"}} and [[Victoria Beckham]] as Queen Amphitrite.{{efn|In the episode "[[The Clash of Triton]]"}}{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref>{{cite news |date=October 11, 2006 |title=David Bowie goes out to sea for 'SpongeBob' |url=http://usatoday30.usatoday.com/life/television/news/2006-10-11-bowie-spongebob_x.htm?csp=27 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191130003017/http://usatoday30.usatoday.com/life/television/news/2006-10-11-bowie-spongebob_x.htm?csp=27 |archive-date=November 30, 2019 |access-date=May 27, 2013 |work=[[USA Today]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=October 11, 2006 |title=Bowie voices SpongeBob character |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/entertainment/6040098.stm |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200916103057/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/entertainment/6040098.stm |archive-date=September 16, 2020 |access-date=May 27, 2013 |publisher=[[BBC]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Moody |first=Annemarie |date=April 1, 2009 |title=Johnny Depp Teaches SpongeBob to Hang Ten in New TV Special |url=http://www.awn.com/news/television/johnny-depp-teaches-spongebob-hang-ten-new-tv-special |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131110234542/http://www.awn.com/news/television/johnny-depp-teaches-spongebob-hang-ten-new-tv-special |archive-date=November 10, 2013 |access-date=May 28, 2013 |publisher=[[Animation World Network]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Thomas |first=Devon |date=June 17, 2010 |title=Victoria Beckham Lends Her Voice to "SpongeBob" |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/victoria-beckham-lends-her-voice-to-spongebob/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131112081319/http://www.cbsnews.com/8301-31749_162-20008078-10391698.html |archive-date=November 12, 2013 |work=[[CBS News]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=July 15, 2010 |title=Victoria Beckham to star in a new episode of ''SpongeBob SquarePants'' |url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/3am/celebrity-news/victoria-beckham-to-star-in-a-new-episode-228859 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170620145930/https://www.mirror.co.uk/3am/celebrity-news/victoria-beckham-to-star-in-a-new-episode-228859 |archive-date=June 20, 2017 |access-date=May 29, 2013 |work=[[Daily Mirror]]}}</ref>}}

Voice recording sessions always include the full cast of actors, which gives ''SpongeBob'' a unique feeling according to Kenny.<ref name="OralHistory"/> For the first three seasons, Hillenburg and Drymon directed the actors. [[Andrea Romano (voice director)|Andrea Romano]] became the voice director in the fourth season, and Kenny took over during the ninth.<ref name="OralHistory"/>

===Animation===
Until at least 2009, the production of ''SpongeBob'' was overseen at [[Nickelodeon Animation Studio]] in Burbank, California, with additional animation services carried out at [[Rough Draft Korea]] in [[Seoul]].{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref name="Childhood" /><ref name="Producing" /><ref>{{cite web |last=Richmond |first=Ray |date=January 15, 2004 |title=Special Report: Animation |url=http://www.roughdraftstudios.com/press/press_20040115.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080310222309/http://www.roughdraftstudios.com/press/press_20040115.html |archive-date=March 10, 2008 |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref>}} The California crew would create storyboards, which would then be used as templates by the crew at Rough Draft, who would animate each scene by hand, color each [[cel]] on computers, and paint backgrounds.<ref name="Childhood" /><ref name="Producing" /> The final stages of production, including editing and adding music, would be completed in California.<ref name=Producing/>

During the first season, the series used [[cel animation]]. A shift was made the following year to [[digital ink and paint]] animation.<ref name="Tibbitt"/> Starting with the [[SpongeBob SquarePants (season 5)|fifth season]] episode "[[Pest of the West]]" (2008), the crew began using [[drawing tablet]]s, which allowed them to draw on computer screens and make immediate changes or undo mistakes. Background designer Kenny Pittenger noted that many of his colleagues did not like the tablets, preferring the immediacy of drawing on paper.<ref name="OralHistory" />

[[File:SpongeBob SquarePants characters by Screen Novelties.jpg|thumb|right|alt=Illustration of the show's character models with SpongeBob on the left | [[Screen Novelties]] created character models based on the works of [[Rankin/Bass Productions|Rankin/Bass]] for the show's stop-motion episodes.]]
Since 2004, the ''SpongeBob'' crew has periodically collaborated with the Los Angeles-based animation studio [[Screen Novelties]] to create [[stop motion|stop-motion]] sequences for special episodes. The studio produced a brief [[clay animation|claymation]] scene for the climax of ''[[The SpongeBob SquarePants Movie]]'', followed by an opening sequence for the series' [[SpongeBob's Truth or Square|tenth anniversary special]] in 2009.{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref name="Huff2">{{cite news |last=Hill |first=Jim |date=November 23, 2012 |title=New ''SpongeBob SquarePants'' Song Urges Shoppers Not to Be Jerks This Holiday Season |url=https://www.huffingtonpost.com/jim-hill/spongebob-christmas-album_b_2175828.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170414081203/http://www.huffingtonpost.com/jim-hill/spongebob-christmas-album_b_2175828.html |archive-date=April 14, 2017 |access-date=April 13, 2017 |work=[[The Huffington Post]]}}</ref><ref name="Production">{{cite web |last=Zahed |first=Ramin |date=November 21, 2012 |title=Stop Motion Casts a Spell on SpongeBob |url=http://www.animationmagazine.net/tv/stop-motion-casts-a-spell-on-spongebob/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121212164211/http://www.animationmagazine.net/tv/stop-motion-casts-a-spell-on-spongebob/ |archive-date=December 12, 2012 |access-date=January 17, 2013 |work=[[Animation Magazine]]}}</ref>{{sfn |Priebe |2011 |pages=61–66}}}} The abominable snow mollusk, an octopus-like creature who acts as the antagonist of the episode "[[Frozen Face-Off]]", was also animated by the company using claymation.<ref name="AWN2012">{{cite web |last=Sarto |first=Dan |date=December 5, 2012 |title=A Stop-Motion SpongeBob Special Means Christmas Comes Early This Year |url=http://www.awn.com/animationworld/stop-motion-spongebob-special-means-christmas-comes-early-year |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141102111308/http://www.awn.com/animationworld/stop-motion-spongebob-special-means-christmas-comes-early-year |archive-date=November 2, 2014 |access-date=April 13, 2017 |work=[[Animation World Network]]}}</ref>

In 2011, the ''SpongeBob'' team asked Screen Novelties to animate an entire episode—the holiday special "It's a SpongeBob Christmas!"—using stop-motion animation. The episode reimagined the show's characters as if they were part of a [[Rankin/Bass Productions|Rankin/Bass]] holiday film.{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref name="Goodman">{{cite news |last=Etkin |first=Jaimie |date=June 14, 2012 |title='SpongeBob SquarePants' Christmas Special: Stop-Motion 'It's A SpongeBob Christmas' With John Goodman |url=https://www.huffingtonpost.com/2012/06/14/spongebob-squarepants-christmas-special_n_1597975.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181024023057/https://www.huffingtonpost.com/2012/06/14/spongebob-squarepants-christmas-special_n_1597975.html |archive-date=October 24, 2018 |access-date=December 14, 2012 |work=[[The Huffington Post]]}}</ref><ref name="AmMagPremiere">{{cite web |last=Zahed |first=Ramin |date=October 18, 2012 |title=CBS Premieres 'It's a SpongeBob Christmas' on Nov. 23 |url=https://www.animationmagazine.net/2012/10/cbs-premieres-its-a-spongebob-christmas-on-nov-23/ |access-date=July 4, 2026 |website=[[Animation Magazine]]}}</ref><ref name="AWNPremiere">{{cite web |last=Washington |first=Ed |date=December 5, 2012 |title=Stop-Motion 'SpongeBob SquarePants' Christmas Special Airs December 6 |url=https://www.awn.com/news/stop-motion-spongebob-squarepants-christmas-special-airs-december-6 |access-date=July 4, 2026 |website=Animation World Network}}</ref><ref name="Huff2"/>}} Unconventional materials such as baking soda, glitter, wood chips, and breakfast cereal were used to create [[Set (film and TV scenery)|sets]] for the episode.<ref name="Production2">{{cite web |last=Beck |first=Jerry |date=November 6, 2012 |title=It's A SpongeBob Stop-Mo Christmas |url=http://www.cartoonbrew.com/stop-motion/its-a-spongebob-stop-mo-christmas-72872.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121113114929/http://www.cartoonbrew.com/stop-motion/its-a-spongebob-stop-mo-christmas-72872.html |archive-date=November 13, 2012 |access-date=January 17, 2013 |work=[[Cartoon Brew]]}}</ref> For their work on the holiday special, Screen Novelties earned one award and two nominations at the [[40th Annie Awards]], a nomination at the 2013 [[Motion Picture Sound Editors|Golden Reel Awards]], and a nomination at the 2013 [[Annecy International Animated Film Festival]].{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref name="Annie40">{{cite web |author=<!--Not stated--> |year=2013 |title=40th Annual Annie Awards Winners |url=http://annieawards.org/40thannieawards/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140218133344/http://www.annieawards.org/40thannieawards |archive-date=February 18, 2014 |work=Annie Award |publisher=[[ASIFA-Hollywood]]}}</ref><ref name="GRA13a">{{cite web |last=Giardina |first=Carolyn |date=January 17, 2013 |title=Sound Editors Announce Golden Reel Nominees |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/race/golden-reel-nominees-413536 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181116035440/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/race/golden-reel-nominees-413536 |archive-date=November 16, 2018 |access-date=April 15, 2013 |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=TV series Official Selection |url=http://www.annecy.org/annecy-2013/festival/official-selection:c8 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130401141556/http://www.annecy.org/annecy-2013/festival/official-selection%3Ac8 |archive-date=April 1, 2013 |publisher=[[Annecy International Animated Film Festival]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Taylor |first=Drew |date=February 5, 2015 |title=Review: Off-The-Wall And Trippy Sequel 'The SpongeBob Movie: Sponge Out Of Water' |url=https://www.indiewire.com/2015/02/review-off-the-wall-and-trippy-sequel-the-spongebob-movie-sponge-out-of-water-267418/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170128162242/https://www.indiewire.com/2015/02/review-off-the-wall-and-trippy-sequel-the-spongebob-movie-sponge-out-of-water-267418/ |archive-date=January 28, 2017 |access-date=April 13, 2017 |work=[[IndieWire]] |publisher=[[Penske Media Corporation]]}}</ref>}} A second stop-motion special, themed around Halloween and using the same Rankin/Bass-inspired character models, was produced for [[SpongeBob SquarePants (season 11)|season 11]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Milligan |first=Mercedes |date=June 20, 2017 |title=Nickelodeon, Screen Novelties Team for SpongeBob Halloween Stop-Mo Special |url=https://www.animationmagazine.net/2017/06/nickelodeon-screen-novelties-team-for-spongebob-halloween-stop-mo-special/ |access-date=July 4, 2026 |website=[[Animation Magazine]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Chitwood |first=Adam |date=October 13, 2017 |title=Go Behind the Scenes of the 'SpongeBob SquarePants' Stop-Motion Halloween Special |url=https://collider.com/spongebob-squarepants-halloween-special-stop-motion-images/ |access-date=July 4, 2026 |website=[[Collider (website)|Collider]]}}</ref>

===Music===
Mark Harrison and Blaise Smith composed the ''SpongeBob SquarePants'' [[SpongeBob SquarePants Theme|theme song]], with lyrics written by Hillenburg and Drymon. The melody was inspired by the sea shanty "[[Blow the Man Down]]".{{Sfn |Neuwirth |2003 |pp=50–51}} The opening sequence of each episode features "Painty the Pirate", a character in an oil painting who sings the theme song.{{Sfn |Neuwirth |2003 |pp=50–51}}<ref name="Radio" /> [[Patrick Pinney]] voices Painty, while Hillenburg's lips are superimposed onto the painting and move along with the lyrics.<ref name="Radio" /> Kenny joked that the lips are the closest fans will ever come to seeing Hillenburg, due to his private nature.{{Sfn |Neuwirth |2003 |pp=50–51}}

A [[Cover (music)|cover]] of the theme song by [[Avril Lavigne]] can be found on [[The SpongeBob SquarePants Movie – Music from the Movie and More...|''The'' ''SpongeBob SquarePants Movie'' soundtrack]].<ref name="Bands">{{cite magazine |last=Mar |first=Alex |date=October 1, 2004 |title=Avril Sings "SpongeBob" |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/news/avril-sings-spongebob-20041001 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130923091020/https://www.rollingstone.com/music/news/avril-sings-spongebob-20041001 |archive-date=September 23, 2013 |access-date=May 22, 2013 |magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=October 1, 2004 |title=Avril Lavigne on ''SpongeBob SquarePants'' |url=http://www.ultimate-guitar.com/news/upcoming_releases/avril_lavigne_on_spongebob_squarepants.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070104005123/http://www.ultimate-guitar.com/news/upcoming_releases/avril_lavigne_on_spongebob_squarepants.html |archive-date=January 4, 2007 |access-date=May 22, 2013 |publisher=[[Ultimate-Guitar]]}}</ref> Another cover by the [[Violent Femmes]] aired on Nickelodeon as a promotion when ''SpongeBob'' moved to [[prime time]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Barker |first=Rayanna |date=June 22, 2001 |title=A Conversation With Brian Ritchie |url=http://www.rockzone.com/interviews/femmes.shtml |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130815213049/http://www.rockzone.com/interviews/femmes.shtml |archive-date=August 15, 2013 |work=Rock Zone}}</ref> Steve Belfer wrote and performed the music heard during the end credits of each episode.<ref name="OralHistory" />

The series' [[Music editor (filmmaking)|music editor]] and main composer is Nicolas Carr. After working with Hillenburg on ''[[Rocko's Modern Life]]'', he struggled to find another job in his field. He was considering a career change before Hillenburg offered him the role of composer for ''SpongeBob''. The first season's score primarily featured selections from the [[APM Music|Associated Production Music Library]] (APML), which Carr has said includes "lots of great old corny Hawaiian music and big, full, dramatic orchestral scores." Carr said he tries to make the show's music "as funny and ridiculous as possible."<ref name="OralHistory" />

Hillenburg felt it was important for the series to develop its own music library, consisting of scores that could be reused and re-edited throughout the years. He wanted these scores to be composed by unknowns, and a group of twelve was assembled. They formed "The Sponge Divers Orchestra", which includes Carr, Belfer, and [[Gary Stockdale]].<ref name=":12">{{Cite web |title=Songview Search |url=https://repertoire.bmi.com/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20001110125600/http://repertoire.bmi.com/ |archive-date=November 10, 2000 |access-date=July 27, 2024 |website=[[Broadcast Music, Inc.|BMI]]}}</ref> The group went on to provide most of the music for later seasons, although Carr still draws from the APML, as well as a library he founded himself—Animation Music Inc.<ref name="OralHistory"/>

